<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Akatziri</h1><p class="st-meta">430 CE – 470 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A steppe people north of the Black Sea who were drawn into Attila's empire and later appear among the forerunners of the Khazars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Akatziri are first mentioned by the Byzantine envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a> in the 440s as a people of the Pontic steppe ruled by many chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> later described them as a strong people who lived by herding and hunting. Their origins and language are unknown; they were probably a Hunnic or early Turkic group.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Around 448 the Eastern Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> tried to bribe their chiefs to turn against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, but the gifts went to the wrong chief, Kuridachus, who betrayed the plan. Attila then subdued them and set his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellac">Ellac</a> over them as king, bringing them firmly into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the collapse of Hun power in the 450s and 460s, the Akatziri disappear from the record, displaced or absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saragurs">Saragurs</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sabirs">Sabirs</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/onogurs">Onogurs</a>. Some historians link them to the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, though the connection is unproven.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/akatziri.jpg" alt="Akatziri" width="320" height="310"><figcaption>Image: Slovenski Volk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huns450.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>430 CE – 470 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kuridachus, Ellac</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>unknown (possibly Hunnic or Turkic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Akatzirs, Agathyrsi (Hunnic)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=450&amp;focus=akatziri" aria-label="Akatziri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5418 446 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Akatziri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5738 702h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5738 702h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ukraine. <a href="/atlas?year=450&amp;focus=akatziri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

